    "mathematics": "Interpret the stated digitwise operation as replacing each decimal digit d of a^2 by the single decimal digit d+5, with positions preserved and no carry or concatenation. Since b has three digits, a^2 must also have three digits and each of its digits must be at most 4; only under these necessary conditions is b=a^2+555. Exhaustively checking a=10,...,99 leaves exactly the six legal transformations (10,100,655), (11,121,676), (12,144,699), (18,324,879), (20,400,955), and (21,441,996), of which only 676 is a square. Independently, write b=m^2. The three-digit condition gives 10<=m<=31, and m^2-a^2=555 gives positive integers x=m-a<y=m+a with xy=555. The complete positive factor-pair list is (1,555),(3,185),(5,111),(15,37); all are odd-odd, so each decodes integrally to (a,m)=(277,278),(91,94),(53,58),(11,26). The constraints 10<=a<=99 and 10<=m<=31 leave only (11,26). Finally 11^2=121, digitwise addition gives 676, and 676=26^2. If one instead allows a digit plus 5 to become a two-character string or introduces carrying, the statement no longer defines the same operation; that alternative is outside the source solution and this lesson's documented domain."
  },
